<Education title="Bring a real question. Use a fictional case." description="A teacher-led introduction to charitable systems, evidence and civic decision-making, with a proposed ages 14–18 adaptation." filePath="src/pages/learn/schools.astro" active="schools" sourceIds={['fcps-partnerships','fcps-expectations','fcps-volunteers','fcps-service-learning','ypi-model','ypi-school-zone']}>
  <a slot="actions" class="btn" href={url('/learn/teach/')}>Read the complete lesson</a><a slot="actions" class="btn btn-ghost" href="#school-materials">Get the school pack</a>
  <section><h2>Begin with an educator’s review.</h2><p>Offer the fictional case and lesson outline to a teacher or authorized school contact. Ask whether the learning question fits an existing subject, enrichment period or extracurricular group. The suggested age range, pace and activities are untested proposals; an educator judges the particular group’s readiness and access needs.</p><p>The material asks learners to distinguish a delivered activity from an observed change, consider what might have happened otherwise and explain a fair disagreement. We have not mapped it to a national, state or school curriculum standard.</p><div class="education-callout"><p><strong>The first activity can stay on paper.</strong> No donation, student account, survey, photograph, public post or personal financial or service history is required. It does not ask pupils to fundraise or select a named charity for an award. Learners can use assigned perspectives and keep their own work.</p></div></section>
  <section><h2>Questions for the school, before a pilot</h2><ul><li>Who owns the lesson and approves any outside contributor or guest visit?</li><li>What supervision, volunteer, room, access and safeguarding arrangements does the school require?</li><li>Does sharing or using a digital resource need a separate review? Can the activity remain offline?</li><li>Who decides whether an activity counts toward any service requirement? This pack awards no hours or credit.</li><li>What feedback can the educator offer without sharing identifiable student work, grades or records?</li></ul><p>As a local example, <a href="https://www.fcps.edu/get-involved/business-and-community-partnerships">Fairfax County Public Schools lists guest speaking, workshops and in-kind enrichment</a>. Its <a href="https://www.fcps.edu/partner-expectations">partner expectations</a> distinguish additional review topics. These pages supply questions to ask staff; they do not approve this lesson or determine which process applies to it.</p><p>A staff-supported club is another possible route. The <a href={url('/learn/clubs/')}>club guide</a> links current public campus and school examples, while leaving unpublished internal requirements unknown.</p></section>
  <section id="school-materials"><p class="eyebrow">Teacher adaptation · editorial draft</p><h2>One taster or four meetings.</h2><p>Use the <a href={url('/learn/teach/')}>50-minute taster</a> as a standalone discussion, or adapt these four meetings. Each proposed timetable totals 50 minutes. Provide the essential figures on paper; fractions and a worked table can replace percentage calculations when useful.</p><TeachingDownloads ids={['school-pack','student-worksheet','facilitator-guide']} /><ol class="education-meeting-list">{beginnerMeetings.map(meeting=><li><h3>{meeting.title}</h3><p>{meeting.activity}</p><p><strong>Leave with:</strong> {meeting.output}</p></li>)}</ol></section>
  <section><h2>Learn from established school programs.</h2><p>The Wood Foundation’s <a href="https://www.thewoodfoundation.org.uk/programmes/ypi">Youth and Philanthropy Initiative in Scotland</a> describes local issue and charity research with presentations. Its <a href="https://ypiscotland.org.uk/school-zone/">School Zone</a> describes teacher planning and reflection. It is a distinct Scottish program; Charitology claims no participation, funding, endorsement or rights to copy its materials.</p><p>Our proposal is to complement existing teaching with a systems-and-evidence exercise. A future evaluation would need its own plan and the institution’s process. An enjoyable lesson, correct arithmetic or a favorable comment does not establish lasting learning or better charitable outcomes.</p></section>
</Education>
